                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "edbf0f77-d930-4b74-9f4c-6d76e3d3819e",
                        "entry_id": "301ab8f1-20b8-367d-b61c-f4e39a0a9bd9",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "tribelets"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "70e9b5f5-2375-3aef-a859-aaa93031e989",
                        "entry_id": "301ab8f1-20b8-367d-b61c-f4e39a0a9bd9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A small tribe of Native Americans, especially a small independent group of Native California people who shared a language and usually comprised one principal village, or several in close proximity, plus smaller resource-gathering camps and territories."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d0a88381-b832-4d9a-a584-689d8d9a967a",
                                "sense_id": "70e9b5f5-2375-3aef-a859-aaa93031e989",
                                "text": "Kroeber’s emphasis on the small scale of indigenous California social organizations led him to attach the diminutive \"-let\" to the anthropologically normative term \"tribe\"."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "be1ef386-6179-47ce-9883-9b99ef4aea15",
                                "sense_id": "70e9b5f5-2375-3aef-a859-aaa93031e989",
                                "text": "The second feature, dialectic separateness, of course is an old story for California, but elsewhere in the state each idiom is usually common to a considerable number of tribelets or \"village communities.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dc14d658-92cc-4df8-8129-4f587b3dfc22",
                                "sense_id": "70e9b5f5-2375-3aef-a859-aaa93031e989",
                                "text": "The larger tribelets usually had several permanent villages."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "27ab09ae-aaba-455b-b564-1caf59a5fab7",
                                "sense_id": "70e9b5f5-2375-3aef-a859-aaa93031e989",
                                "text": "Tribelet […] defined a political and geographical unit comprising several units, usually including a principal and most powerful central village, tied by relations of kinship."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "22e0cf2d-8b64-3847-9b78-9a37ce781711",
                        "entry_id": "301ab8f1-20b8-367d-b61c-f4e39a0a9bd9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A small tribal society in another part of the world."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0c805fc7-25dc-467d-acc9-c36706f7ae8b",
                                "sense_id": "22e0cf2d-8b64-3847-9b78-9a37ce781711",
                                "text": "[…] if each tribelet was linguistically quite independent, this would have made communication in the New World a matter of the utmost difficulty for the [enslaved Africans], who would have been far more dependent than otherwise on the entirely new language that had to be learned."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
